[FINAL-TEXT] **Root cause found.** Router6's FORWARD chain has explicit DROP rules for all traffic entering on **eth2** (the WAN uplink to router3):

```
iifname "eth2" ip saddr 172.16.10.0/24 ip daddr 172.18.51.1 tcp dport 80 ACCEPT  ← lone exception
iifname "eth2" tcp → DROP  (0 pkts)
iifname "eth2" udp → DROP  (15 pkts = the probe pings!)
iifname "eth2" all → DROP  (24 pkts = ICMP from traceroutes!)
```

The counters prove active drops. Router6 can self-originate pings to pc16 (not subject to FORWARD), but all *forwarded* packets arriving from the WAN (router3 side) are silently dropped. This kills pc4→pc16 in the forward direction, AND kills pc15→pc12 on the return trip (pc12's reply re-enters router6 via eth2 and is dropped).

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The `filter` table `FORWARD` chain in iptables/nft on router6, specifically targeting its eth2 interface (the r6_r3 WAN uplink to router3).

**Mechanism:** Router6 has four FORWARD rules bound to inbound interface eth2. The first grants a narrow exception (TCP port 80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1). The next three rules — `drop all TCP`, `drop all UDP`, `drop all remaining` — catch everything else. Any packet from the rest of the network destined for the 172.18.51.x/172.18.50.x (Location C) subnets must transit router6 via eth2; those packets are silently discarded. Simultaneously, return traffic from Location C hosts outbound to the wider network also passes back through router6.eth2 on re-entry (e.g., pc12's reply to a pc15-initiated ping), and is likewise dropped. Packet counters in the DROP rules (15 UDP, 24 ICMP packets matched) confirm the rules are actively firing.

**Localization:** Router6, iptables/nft `filter FORWARD`, `iifname "eth2"` catch-all DROP rules.

**Root cause:** A restrictive inbound ACL policy on router6's WAN interface (eth2) blocks all forwarded traffic arriving from router3, effectively isolating the entire 172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 (Location C) subnet cluster from every other network segment.

**Fix:** Remove the three catch-all DROP rules from router6's FORWARD chain (`nft flush chain ip filter FORWARD` or the equivalent `iptables -F FORWARD` after backing up the intended narrow exception), then re-add only the legitimate permit rules if any are required by policy.
=== END ===